  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Useful mainly as a composition envelope rather than a full corrupt-chunk protocol. It cleanly separates envelope dispatch from payload-level CID/hash verification and can preserve signed local evidence, but rejection accounting, retry rules, and bad-chunk attribution conventions are still left to surrounding protocols.",
    "strengths": [
      "Clearly keeps content-addressed chunk rules in the payload protocol instead of requiring central enforcement in the envelope.",
      "Mandatory signature slots plus canonical DAG-CBOR bytes support repeatable local evidence about what bytes were sent.",
      "Unknown pCID handling requires unsupported/unverified marking instead of silent interpretation."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "It does not natively define CID mismatch checks, peer-local accounting records, or retry behavior.",
      "Relay and accounting evidence may live in wrappers, payloads, or signature slots, so corrupt-chunk attribution is not fully pinned down.",
      "As a draft specimen with opaque positional payload bytes, it is only partly auditable without the payload protocol."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "A valid envelope signature could be mistaken for proof that the chunk matches its claimed CID.",
      "Best-effort exposure of unsupported payload bytes could encourage unsafe or inconsistent salvage tooling."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Which payload or feed protocol records corrupt-chunk rejection and retry evidence locally?",
      "What exact local accounting record ties Mallory's bad bytes to the failed CID claim?",
      "Should unknown-pCID best-effort inspection be further constrained when corruption is suspected?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
}
